WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.800 position:63%
Adobe has a problem. For years, they’ve&nbsp;
made you rent their apps. $105 a month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.700 position:63%
forever. And if you try to cancel early…&nbsp;
that’ll cost you too. But one developer cloned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:20.300 position:63%
it in under a week. And gave it away for free.
Then Premiere. Then Lightroom. Then Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:27.800 position:63%
Word, Excel, PowerPoint. For as long as software&nbsp;
has existed, the hard part was building it. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:35.000 position:63%
now, software has changed. And there’s no&nbsp;
going back. Yet this gets even weirder.&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.300 position:63%
Most software stocks collapsed, but&nbsp;
have gradually rebounded… except Adobe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:56.900 position:63%
Even with record revenue. Can Adobe survive?
Ok, first, what the heck are these clones things?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:03.600 position:63%
Well, let’s get a bit more context. Photoshop,&nbsp;
Premiere Pro, Lightroom. These are some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:10.800 position:63%
Adobe's biggest products, and a single app costs&nbsp;
around $23 a month, or as high as $105 a month for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.300 position:63%
Creative Cloud. This has been one of the most&nbsp;
lucrative, dominant software empires on earth.&nbsp;

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.100 position:63%
Together, Adobe's creative and marketing software&nbsp;
brought in $4.65 billion in just the last three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:28.800 position:63%
months. Which brings us to their clones, and&nbsp;
developer Brandon Thomas. What motivated him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.600 position:63%
to do all of this… is quite ironic.
Brandon Thomas announced seven free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.800 position:63%
open-source alternatives to Adobe's creative&nbsp;
software, on GitHub. This came with a promise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:44.400 position:63%
of “100% parity within one month,” which simply&nbsp;
means to match the originals completely within a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:51.800 position:63%
month. But would he actually achieve this?… More&nbsp;
on that later. He called the project ArtCraft.&nbsp;

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:58.400 position:63%
Photoshop became PhotoCraft, Illustrator became&nbsp;
VectorCraft, and Premiere Pro became FilmCraft.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.693 position:63%
You get the idea. They were designed to&nbsp;
be functional clones of Adobe's apps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.693 --> 00:02:08.293 position:63%
with nearly identical features. Thomas believes&nbsp;
AI can now build software that once took teams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.293 --> 00:02:14.993 position:63%
of engineers months or even years to create.
Not only that, they’re open source. Anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.993 --> 00:02:20.493 position:63%
could copy, modify, and even sell them. And they&nbsp;
could run on Windows, Mac, Linux, or straight from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.493 --> 00:02:28.193 position:63%
a browser. So, how did this happen, and why now?
On September 22, Anthropic released Claude Opus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.193 --> 00:02:33.793 position:63%
5.5, the AI model Thomas would use to&nbsp;
build these clones. Eight days later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.793 --> 00:02:39.493 position:63%
PhotoCraft's first code appeared on GitHub.&nbsp;
He used eight AI agents working in parallel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.493 --> 00:02:45.193 position:63%
to write code that recreated how Adobe's software&nbsp;
worked without copying its original source code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.193 --> 00:02:51.493 position:63%
something called a clean-room rebuild. By October&nbsp;
1, it had gone from supporting 224 Photoshop menu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.493 --> 00:03:01.193 position:63%
items to 532 out of 625 in a single day.&nbsp;
Two days later, it was 625. On October 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.193 --> 00:03:06.093 position:63%
Thomas released the first signed version.
And ArtCraft drew a lot of attention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.593 --> 00:03:14.293 position:63%
PhotoCraft had 6,691 GitHub stars on October 7,&nbsp;
essentially bookmarks or likes for GitHub. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:21.593 position:63%
passed 23,000 the next day, 44,000 by October&nbsp;
14, and, as of writing, it’s over 74,000.&nbsp;

00:03:21.593 --> 00:03:27.893 position:63%
… That’s a lot. So, why do this?&nbsp;
Why go to all this trouble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.893 --> 00:03:33.893 position:63%
and release it for free? Well, that’s what&nbsp;
I find extremely ironic, and kinda funny.&nbsp;

00:03:34.493 --> 00:03:39.093 position:63%
If you’ve watched our past videos, you’d&nbsp;
know about the dreaded Adobe cancellation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.093 --> 00:03:43.893 position:63%
fee. The reason so many people loathe this&nbsp;
company, and why the US government sued them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.893 --> 00:03:50.293 position:63%
and why Adobe had to pay $150 million in fines&nbsp;
and refunds. Well... Thomas is one of them.&nbsp;

00:03:50.793 --> 00:03:54.493 position:63%
"They charged me the 'cancellation&nbsp;
fee' on multiple occasions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.493 --> 00:04:00.493 position:63%
and I've hated them ever since,"
In fact, this was one of his selling points:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.493 --> 00:04:07.094 position:63%
"No more subscriptions with cancellation fees."
But he didn't stop at Adobe. Within the same week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.094 --> 00:04:12.294 position:63%
three more projects appeared on GitHub:&nbsp;
WordCraft, GridCraft, and DeckCraft. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.294 --> 00:04:18.894 position:63%
were free alternatives to Microsoft Word, Excel,&nbsp;
and PowerPoint. Wordcraft pulled in about 14,700&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.894 --> 00:04:23.994 position:63%
GitHub stars within the month of release.
It is, very funny, that Adobe’s predatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.994 --> 00:04:30.694 position:63%
cancellation fees caused these clone products to&nbsp;
appear… but it’s not the only reason. Thomas has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.694 --> 00:04:34.994 position:63%
another motivation for building these.&nbsp;
And more importantly, ArtCraft is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.994 --> 00:04:40.394 position:63%
a tiny part of a major shift in software.
Adobe isn't the only company facing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.394 --> 00:04:45.394 position:63%
problem. Software has changed, and I&nbsp;
don't think there's any going back.

00:04:53.794 --> 00:04:59.194 position:63%
Over time, online businesses and e-commerce have&nbsp;
become more refined. There's a proven effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.194 --> 00:05:04.294 position:63%
way of doing things, checkouts, page layouts,&nbsp;
marketable products. A lot of the problem is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.294 --> 00:05:09.594 position:63%
figuring all of that out and implementing it&nbsp;
manually. Don't let that be a bottleneck. Jump&nbsp;&nbsp;

