00:06:25.996 --> 00:06:31.696 position:63%
I want to talk about Thomas a bit more, and&nbsp;
I’m not saying he was being untruthful earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.696 --> 00:06:38.096 position:63%
but there’s some extra context we need. His&nbsp;
Reddit account, u/ai_art_is_art, offers us a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.096 --> 00:06:44.496 position:63%
few clues already. Thomas has around 15 years of&nbsp;
engineering experience, including time at Square.&nbsp;

00:06:44.496 --> 00:06:51.296 position:63%
More importantly, he is… or was the CEO of&nbsp;
Storyteller.ai, the company behind FakeYou,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.296 --> 00:06:56.596 position:63%
a service that could generate realistic imitations&nbsp;
of celebrity voices. Which is a huge, ongoing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.596 --> 00:07:03.396 position:63%
problem with AI. In October 2022, Italy's privacy&nbsp;
watchdog opened an inquiry into FakeYou over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.396 --> 00:07:10.296 position:63%
concerns about the potential misuse of people's&nbsp;
voices. But Storyteller.ai now has a new name:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.296 --> 00:07:15.696 position:63%
ArtCraft. And it turns out these free apps&nbsp;
seem to be funnels into his other AI business.&nbsp;

00:07:15.696 --> 00:07:21.996 position:63%
For image and video generation, ArtCraft sells&nbsp;
credits for $10, $35, or $60 a month. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.996 --> 00:07:26.396 position:63%
revenue from this business helps fund the&nbsp;
development of the free software. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.396 --> 00:07:31.096 position:63%
you can see how the two businesses connect.&nbsp;
Inside the free apps, users can buy credits to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.096 --> 00:07:37.096 position:63%
access ArtCraft's own AI models, alongside models&nbsp;
from other providers. And if you visit any of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.096 --> 00:07:42.696 position:63%
apps' GitHub pages, you'll find another link&nbsp;
pointing straight to ArtCraft's paid AI studio.&nbsp;

00:07:42.696 --> 00:07:48.996 position:63%
But it's not just a funnel, because you don't&nbsp;
actually have to pay for anything. The editing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.996 --> 00:07:54.696 position:63%
tools remain free, and you can use them without&nbsp;
buying a single AI credit. Thomas isn’t alone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.696 --> 00:08:00.396 position:63%
or particularly nefarious either. This is how&nbsp;
many AI companies operate. At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.396 --> 00:08:05.496 position:63%
the license lets anyone copy the code and&nbsp;
build their own free apps. They just have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.496 --> 00:08:11.597 position:63%
to remove ArtCraft's branding. In fact,&nbsp;
there are already around 12,100 copies.&nbsp;

00:08:11.597 --> 00:08:17.297 position:63%
But a lot of this hinges on one question:&nbsp;
Do these clone apps actually work? And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.297 --> 00:08:22.197 position:63%
answer is... kind of. I've tested&nbsp;
PhotoCraft and some of the others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.197 --> 00:08:26.797 position:63%
and one thing that stands out is how lightweight&nbsp;
it feels compared with Photoshop, which takes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.797 --> 00:08:32.097 position:63%
million years to open. But being lightweight&nbsp;
doesn't automatically make it a replacement.&nbsp;

00:08:32.697 --> 00:08:38.097 position:63%
Remember what they said about “100% parity”?&nbsp;
Well, that promise slowly began to change.&nbsp;

00:08:38.697 --> 00:08:44.597 position:63%
"99% parity will take a while, but I'm sure&nbsp;
it'll be measured in months and not years."&nbsp;

00:08:45.297 --> 00:08:51.797 position:63%
So… not 100% within a month. On October&nbsp;
5, PhotoCraft rated itself just 25 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.797 --> 00:08:58.597 position:63%
35% ready for real work. By October 10,&nbsp;
that figure had risen to around 45%. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.597 --> 00:09:03.597 position:63%
the developers acknowledged the limitations.
"It is not yet a Photoshop replacement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.597 --> 00:09:07.497 position:63%
for daily professional work."
PDFs, for example, aren’t reliably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.497 --> 00:09:12.897 position:63%
accepted yet. So… not a perfect clone.
The Office clones have similar problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.897 --> 00:09:19.397 position:63%
too. WordCraft rated itself around 60% ready,&nbsp;
but charts and SmartArt graphics still wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.397 --> 00:09:26.097 position:63%
survive a save properly. GridCraft was around&nbsp;
50% ready, while DeckCraft sat at roughly 55%.&nbsp;

00:09:26.697 --> 00:09:32.697 position:63%
But the bigger question is: what did Adobe do?&nbsp;
These are very clearly clones of their products.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.297 --> 00:09:40.797 position:63%
Did they sue? Send a DMCA? Well, no. They’ve&nbsp;
done… nothing? As of writing, there’s no response.&nbsp;

00:09:41.397 --> 00:09:46.497 position:63%
“I’m just surprised that Adobe hasn’t issued&nbsp;
a cease and desist yet, although I suspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.497 --> 00:09:50.097 position:63%
that lawyers for Adobe realise that this&nbsp;
could end up being a game of whack-a-mole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.097 --> 00:09:55.897 position:63%
if they don’t address the root cause.”
Granted, lawsuits and responses do take time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.897 --> 00:10:01.597 position:63%
But there’s a chance they don’t respond at all.
First of all, copying what software does isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.597 --> 00:10:08.197 position:63%
automatically illegal. In the 1995 case Lotus&nbsp;
v. Borland, a US appeals court ruled that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.197 --> 00:10:12.998 position:63%
software menu system wasn't protected by&nbsp;
copyright, comparing it to the buttons on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.998 --> 00:10:19.098 position:63%
VCR. The code itself is a different story.&nbsp;
That can be copyrighted. But remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.098 --> 00:10:25.198 position:63%
ArtCraft was reverse engineered. The end&nbsp;
result is the same, but not the way it’s built.&nbsp;

00:10:25.198 --> 00:10:29.798 position:63%
One potential issue is something called&nbsp;
trade dress: copying a product's overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.798 --> 00:10:34.698 position:63%
look closely enough that people might confuse&nbsp;
it with the original. PhotoCraft's own README&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.698 --> 00:10:40.298 position:63%
makes the resemblance pretty clear: "If you&nbsp;
know Photoshop, you already know PhotoCraft."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.298 --> 00:10:45.898 position:63%
But ironically this brings us to something&nbsp;
big in Adobe’s favor. At least, for now.&nbsp;

00:10:46.598 --> 00:10:52.198 position:63%
Take photographers, who use Lightroom. LightCraft&nbsp;
can import their Lightroom catalogs and presets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.198 --> 00:10:57.498 position:63%
but their edits won’t look the same.&nbsp;
Then there’s all their paid LUTs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.498 --> 00:11:03.298 position:63%
as well as paid plugins for Photoshop, which don’t&nbsp;
work in PhotoCraft. Adobe has color profiles for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.298 --> 00:11:10.298 position:63%
about 1,500 cameras. LightCraft has four. And some&nbsp;
Canon and Olympus RAW files won’t open at all.&nbsp;

00:11:10.298 --> 00:11:16.798 position:63%
Then there's trust. For example, photographer Glyn&nbsp;
Dewis, who discloses paid work for Adobe, said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.798 --> 00:11:22.398 position:63%
he wouldn't install PhotoCraft, citing its early&nbsp;
alpha state and Thomas's hostility toward Adobe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.098 --> 00:11:28.098 position:63%
Jumping to a new, potentially unstable clone…&nbsp;
for something you use for your livelihood? Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.098 --> 00:11:33.098 position:63%
you use for professional, high-paying clients? I&nbsp;
just don’t see a lot of professionals switching.&nbsp;

00:11:33.098 --> 00:11:38.698 position:63%
Even if Adobe is a big company with dubious&nbsp;
morals… It's still a big company, which comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.698 --> 00:11:43.598 position:63%
with trust and stability. I can’t imagine&nbsp;
any giant companies rushing to replace Adobe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.598 --> 00:11:49.098 position:63%
either. Workers also carry existing knowledge and&nbsp;
expertise and procedures that include software.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.798 --> 00:11:55.398 position:63%
Breaking that takes a lot of time to fix.
But… some definitely will. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.398 --> 00:11:58.898 position:63%
the unavoidable problem Adobe&nbsp;
and many others have to face.&nbsp;

00:11:59.698 --> 00:12:06.398 position:63%
To many, this isn’t actually a surprise. Some&nbsp;
saw this coming, but, what is really surprising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.398 --> 00:12:11.698 position:63%
is that Adobe’s stock is struggling, while other&nbsp;
SaaS companies have rebounded. Why might Adobe be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.698 --> 00:12:26.099 position:63%
hurt the most by this new era of software cloning?
If you’ve watched this far, consider subscribing!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.900 position:63%
It helps the channel and means&nbsp;
you won’t miss future videos.&nbsp;

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:35.300 position:63%
This is bigger than Adobe. Wall Street&nbsp;
saw it coming. In February 2026,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:40.000 position:63%
Anthropic released plugins for Claude Cowork,&nbsp;
and software stocks took a hit. Often called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:44.700 position:63%
the "SaaSpocalypse." Why keep paying for&nbsp;
software when AI could help companies build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:51.100 position:63%
their own? The S&amp;P 500 software index fell&nbsp;
more than 26% between late January and April.&nbsp;

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:55.900 position:63%
But then, the panic started to fade. By&nbsp;
October 6, the index had climbed to its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.900 --> 00:13:02.300 position:63%
highest level since November 2025. …Except&nbsp;
Adobe. Which was still down 31% for the year.&nbsp;

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:07.200 position:63%
"The whole SaaSpocalypse didn't happen&nbsp;
anywhere near as fast as some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:12.500 position:63%
people on Wall Street thought it would."
So why Adobe? In September, the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:19.800 position:63%
reported a record $6.76 billion in quarterly&nbsp;
revenue. Well, there’s a big problem. The money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:25.100 position:63%
it added from new subscriptions was down&nbsp;
about 38% compared with the year before.&nbsp;

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:31.700 position:63%
Investors price a company on where growth is&nbsp;
heading, and that number points down. By October,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:37.800 position:63%
Wells Fargo had downgraded Adobe, cutting&nbsp;
the price target from $270 to $225,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:42.800 position:63%
warning that "cracks are starting to show." Its&nbsp;
survey of IT leaders found Adobe was "a clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:49.100 position:63%
loser of budget share" as companies shifted money&nbsp;
toward AI. So the amount companies would budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:55.600 position:63%
for Adobe is shrinking. Meaning the total pool&nbsp;
of potential revenue or growth is shrinking.&nbsp;

00:13:55.600 --> 00:14:01.500 position:63%
And right now, companies are already building&nbsp;
their own software. In a survey of 817 people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:07.000 position:63%
who build or buy software, 35% had already&nbsp;
replaced at least one paid tool with something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:14.300 position:63%
they built themselves. And 78% expected to build&nbsp;
even more in 2026. Even Starbucks is getting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:20.501 position:63%
on it. The company spends around $400 million a&nbsp;
year on software, but now is building some with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.501 --> 00:14:24.201 position:63%
AI to cut down on that cost.
"There's clear opportunities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.201 --> 00:14:29.001 position:63%
to reduce the spend in software."
And PhotoCraft is just one of several AI-built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.001 --> 00:14:34.101 position:63%
alternatives that have appeared. One developer&nbsp;
behind another clone reportedly spent around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.101 --> 00:14:40.701 position:63%
$2,000 in AI fees to build it. Though, sometimes,&nbsp;
trying to replace existing tools can backfire.&nbsp;

00:14:40.701 --> 00:14:46.901 position:63%
Back in August 2024, Klarna's CEO announced that&nbsp;
the company was dropping Salesforce and Workday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.901 --> 00:14:52.201 position:63%
crediting AI for helping it do more with less.
"How is he achieving compliance, governance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.201 --> 00:14:59.701 position:63%
his company? What is his institutional memory?"
And… yeah, less than a year later, it fizzled out.&nbsp;

00:14:59.701 --> 00:15:05.001 position:63%
"We did not replace SaaS with an LLM."
So, where does this leave Adobe?&nbsp;

00:15:05.001 --> 00:15:10.501 position:63%
There will always be lock-in a clone can't&nbsp;
copy. But that only applies to people and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.501 --> 00:15:15.901 position:63%
companies already using Adobe. What about&nbsp;
someone just starting out? What about new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.901 --> 00:15:20.801 position:63%
businesses? Why would they pay for Photoshop&nbsp;
if a free alternative could do the job?&nbsp;

00:15:20.801 --> 00:15:27.801 position:63%
Then again, free alternatives aren't exactly&nbsp;
new. GIMP has been free since the 1990s. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.801 --> 00:15:32.601 position:63%
in October 2025, Canva made Affinity,&nbsp;
another Photoshop competitor, free.&nbsp;

00:15:33.201 --> 00:15:37.701 position:63%
But history also shows us what can happen&nbsp;
when a smaller company successfully copies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.701 --> 00:15:44.101 position:63%
a giant. In 1982, everyone wanted IBM’s&nbsp;
new PC. But it was built mostly from parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.101 --> 00:15:49.501 position:63%
anyone could buy. The only piece rivals&nbsp;
couldn’t copy was IBM’s startup software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.501 --> 00:15:54.801 position:63%
called the BIOS. But Compaq rebuilt it from&nbsp;
scratch in nine months, for about $1 million.&nbsp;

00:15:54.801 --> 00:16:00.901 position:63%
And… IBM never sued. Within a few years,&nbsp;
IBM began falling behind, in the market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.901 --> 00:16:06.101 position:63%
it had entirely controlled just a few years&nbsp;
earlier. Thomas uses this example himself.&nbsp;

00:16:07.001 --> 00:16:12.501 position:63%
Some analysts believe the real test for software&nbsp;
companies could come in the second half of 2027,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.501 --> 00:16:18.002 position:63%
when more powerful AI coding tools become&nbsp;
available. Till then, we'll have to wait and see.&nbsp;

00:16:18.603 --> 00:16:24.802 position:63%
Either way, Adobe has reason to worry,&nbsp;
but it’s not just from AI software.&nbsp;

00:16:24.802 --> 00:16:28.902 position:63%
On another front, editors and filmmakers&nbsp;
are fleeing to a rival that’s winning by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.903 --> 00:16:34.603 position:63%
giving customers the one thing Adobe won’t:&nbsp;
full ownership of their software. Pay once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.603 --> 00:16:42.103 position:63%
own forever. Watch this video next&nbsp;
to learn the rest of the story.

